  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CDT Wed Jul 15 2026\n\nWildfire smoke:\n\nSmoke from wildfires to the north along the Minnesota/Ontario border \nnorthward into Canada has been sinking southward behind a weak \nboundary today resulting in visibility under 1 miles in spots. \nIn addition, an Air Quality Advisory remains in effect from the\nWisconsin DNR due to the impacts from smoke into at least \nThursday.\n\nThis smoke is likely to persist over the area through late week.\nAlthough there will be a few periods ahead of frontal passages \non Friday and Monday where low-level winds shift more southerly,\nprevailing northwest flow aloft over the next week will favor \ncontinued transport of smoke southeast with any active fires to \nthe north.\n\nThunderstorm chances:\n\nOverall, thunderstorm chances will trend upward over the next few \ndays. There is a very small chance (<10%) for a shower or storm\nacross central Wisconsin this afternoon within the stronger \ninstability along the surface boundary, although forcing for \nascent and boundary layer convergence is weak. \n\nGreater instability will be focused across southwest Wisconsin\non Thursday. A few thunderstorms may develop (20-30% chance) \nacross central Wisconsin during the afternoon in proximity to\nthe instability, but given the limited forcing for ascent and \nmodest deep layer shear, the severe weather risk is very low. \n\nThe two periods with the highest thunderstorm chances are Friday\ninto Friday night and again Monday as fronts swing through. The\ngreatest overlap of shear/instability for any stronger storms\nlooks to be across northern Wisconsin for Friday. Will have to\nwatch trends with smoke across the region, which may impact the\nmagnitude of destabilization.\n\nPredictability is more limited on Monday given differences in \nthe timing and placement of embedded shortwave troughs and \nsurface boundaries. However, there are medium (40-60%) chances \nfor showers and storms. With somewhat stronger northwest mid- \nlevel flow, the environment would support organized storms, but\nconfidence in severe storms is low at this time.\n\nTemperatures:\n\nTemperatures will remain above average into early next week,\nalthough cooler than prior days. Peak heat indices at times may\nreach 90 in spots but thicker smoke may limit daytime \ntemperatures and heat indices to some degree over the next few\ndays.  \n\nA cooler, drier airmass is favored by the middle of next week\nwithin the deeper northwesterly flow aloft.",
